<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Matteo Ricci</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1552">1552</a>–<a href="/years/1610">1610</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Matthaeus Riccius, Li Madou</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Matteo Ricci was an Italian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a>, born in Macerata in the Papal States, who served at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and was one of the founders of the Jesuit China missions. Arriving at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macau">Macau</a> in 1582, he mastered the Chinese language and script, settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhaoqing">Zhaoqing</a> and later reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>, and in 1601 became the first European invited into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forbidden-city">Forbidden City</a>, valued for his predictions of solar eclipses. He stressed parallels between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a>, converted several officials, and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xu-guangqi">Xu Guangqi</a> translated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euclids-elements">Euclid's Elements</a> into Chinese. He made the Kunyu Wanguo Quantu world map of 1602, and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> in 1610, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wanli-emperor">Wanli Emperor</a> granted him a burial place.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/matteo-ricci.jpg" alt="Matteo Ricci" width="330" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chinese brother Emmanuel Pereira (born Yu Wen-hui 游文辉) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:利玛窦像，游文辉绘.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Matteo Ricci was born on 6 October 1552 in Macerata. He studied law in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> for two years and entered the Society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> at the Roman College in April 1571, where he also studied mathematics, cosmology and astronomy under Christopher Clavius. He sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> in March 1578 and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a> that September, and in 1582 he was summoned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macau">Macau</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1583 Ricci and Michele Ruggieri settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhaoqing">Zhaoqing</a> at the invitation of the governor Wang Pan. He stayed until 1589, when a new viceroy expelled him. In 1584 he composed a first European-style world map in Chinese, and the two men are thought to have compiled the first Portuguese-Chinese dictionary. He reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanchang">Nanchang</a> in 1595, and in August 1597 his superior Alessandro Valignano appointed him head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> mission. He first reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> on 7 September 1598 but could not enter the palace.</p><p>In 1601 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wanli-emperor">Wanli Emperor</a> invited him to the court in recognition of his scientific skill, chiefly his predictions of solar eclipses. He had free access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forbidden-city">Forbidden City</a> but never met the emperor, who gave him a stipend. He founded the Cathedral of the Immaculate Conception, the oldest Catholic church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, and converted a number of officials, the most prominent being the agronomist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xu-guangqi">Xu Guangqi</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Ricci adopted the dress of the Confucian literati and argued that Chinese people had always believed in God and that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> completed their faith. He used the term Tiānzhǔ, Lord of Heaven, for God, and took an accommodating view of rites such as ancestor worship, which Dominican and Franciscan missionaries later challenged in the Chinese rites controversy. His book The True Meaning of the Lord of Heaven argued that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a> and Christianity were not opposed, and his Chinese Euclid appeared in 1607.</p><p>Ricci died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> on 11 May 1610. Ming law required foreigners who died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> to be buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macau">Macau</a>, but Diego de Pantoja pleaded for a plot, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wanli-emperor">Wanli Emperor</a> granted one at a Buddhist temple. His journals were edited by Nicolas Trigault and published in 1615.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Matteo Ricci” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the late Ming the Jesuits arrived, the most influential of them <b>Matteo Ricci</b>, who worked with the convert Xu Guangqi; the Nanjing incident of 1616 led to the exile of four Jesuits to Macau.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ming-china">Social Hierarchy of Ming China</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Macerata, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.3,13.45&amp;year=1589" aria-label="Macerata on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4874 627 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Matteo Ricci"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5324 897h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5324 897h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.3,13.45&amp;year=1589">What was here in 1589? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
